\title[The Posterior Filter Equals the Sham]{The Posterior Filter Equals the Sham:\\ Public-Residue Information Has Zero Conversion\\ into Trial-Division Speedup}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Public quantities computable from a semiprime $N=pq$ --- residues of $N$ modulo small integers, quadratic characters, Frobenius classes --- carry genuine information about the factor pair: a battery of such ``dials'' was measured at $3.49$ bits. We ask whether this information can be converted into a speedup of trial division, and answer \emph{no}, both experimentally and exactly. Experimentally, a Bayesian candidate filter built from the exact posterior over the smaller factor's residue class is indistinguishable from a same-size coin-flip keep-set at every dial (maximum discrepancy $0.0075$ against a batch standard deviation of $0.0073$, on $20{,}000$ semiprimes per cell in five batches), and under honest accounting every filter, real or sham, runs at about $0.50\times$ the speed of plain trial division. Theoretically, in the residue model where the two factor residues are independent and uniform in a finite group $G$, we prove: a flat-posterior theorem; the keep-rate law (a filter's success count is exactly its total keep size, so the real filter and any same-size sham are identical); exact no-fallback failure probability $1/|G|$; ordering invariance; a $1\times$ cap and an exact $0.5\times$ law when membership tests are priced at one division; sham co-inflation of unpriced-test bugs; $N$-blind optimality under arbitrary target priors; the exact boundary of the sham law (the unordered event); and the information-theoretic statement that every public dial carries zero bits about the target and its full entropy about the pair.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Trial division finds the smaller prime factor $p$ of $N=pq$ by testing candidate divisors up to $\sqrt N$; its cost is the number of candidates examined. A natural hope is to prune the candidate list using information that $N$ itself reveals. Such information exists: if $3\nmid pq$, then $N\bmod 3$ reveals whether $p\equiv q\pmod 3$, and analogous statements hold modulo any $m$. We call a function of $N$ alone a \emph{dial}, and a tuple of dials a \emph{battery}. The \emph{type channel} --- the information public dials carry about the factor pair --- was measured carefully, and a battery reaching $3.49$ bits was assembled. The open question was one of \emph{utility}: how much of this capacity converts into factoring speed? A speedup of $4/3\times$ was predicted before the decisive experiment.

This paper reports the experiment and the theory explaining its outcome: \emph{the real posterior filter equals the sham, and the conversion rate is exactly zero.} The mechanism is one change of variables. For independent uniform residues $a,b$ in a finite group, $(a,b)\mapsto(a,ab)$ is a bijection of $G\times G$; hence the \emph{target} residue $a$ and the \emph{public} residue $ab$ are independent. The public number constrains the pair heavily but leaves the marginal of the target perfectly flat. We call this \emph{barrier~2}; every filtering, ordering and cost statement below follows from it exactly.

\section{The experiment}

\subsection{Design}
For each dial --- $d_2$ (a $1$-bit dial), $d_3$, $d_5$, and two batteries $\mathrm{bat}_2$, $\mathrm{bat}_4$ (the latter carrying $3.49$ bits about the factor pair):
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item the exact posterior of the smaller factor's residue class given the dial reading was computed;
\item the \emph{real filter} keeps, for each reading, the most probable classes (a fraction $\rho$ of all classes) and trial-divides only by candidates in kept classes;
\item the \emph{sham filter} keeps a set of the same size chosen by independent coin flips;
\item success and cost were measured on $20{,}000$ random semiprimes per cell in five independent batches, with a fixed seed;
\item the keep rate $\rho$ was swept over a grid.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Results}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Real $=$ sham.} At every dial, $\max|\text{real}-\text{sham}|=0.0075$, against a batch standard deviation of $0.0073$.
\item \textbf{Keep rate only.} On the $\rho$-grid, success of both filters is a function of $\rho$ alone.
\item \textbf{No-fallback failures.} Discarding one class without fallback fails at rate exactly $1/n$.
\item \textbf{Honest cost.} Pricing each membership test at one division on every candidate up to $\sqrt N$, every filter runs at $\approx0.50\times$: a net factor-of-two loss.
\item \textbf{Prediction.} The $4/3\times$ prediction is refuted; under complete-procedure accounting the sharp cap is $1\times$.
\end{itemize}

\section{The residue model}

Throughout, $G$ is a finite group of order $n$, e.g.\ $G=(\Z/m\Z)^\times$. The target $p$ and cofactor $q$ are recorded by residues $a,b\in G$.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Equidistribution hypothesis.} $(a,b)$ is uniform on $G\times G$. The public number fixes the \emph{public residue} $c=ab$.

\begin{definition}[Dial]
A \emph{public dial} is any function $\varphi:G\to D$ to a finite set, evaluated at $c$. A battery $(\varphi_1,\varphi_2)$ is again a dial.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Filter]
A \emph{filter} is a map $K:G\to2^G$. It \emph{succeeds} on $(a,b)$ when $a\in K(ab)$. Its \emph{success count} is
$\hit(K)=\#\{(a,b)\in G\times G: a\in K(ab)\}$. It has \emph{constant keep size} $k$ if $|K(c)|=k$ for all $c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ordering policy]
An \emph{ordering policy} assigns to each $c$ a bijection $r_c:G\to\{0,\dots,n-1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Flat transport]\label{lem:transport}
For any $f:G\times G\to M$ into an additive commutative monoid,
\[\sum_{(a,b)\in G\times G} f(a,ab)=\sum_{c\in G}\sum_{a\in G} f(a,c).\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For fixed $a$, $b\mapsto ab$ is a bijection of $G$, so $\sum_b f(a,ab)=\sum_c f(a,c)$. Sum over $a$ and exchange the order of summation.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Only left multiplication is used; commutativity is never needed.
\end{remark}

\section{The flat posterior (barrier 2)}

\begin{theorem}[Flat posterior]\label{thm:flat}
For every dial $\varphi$, reading $d$ and class $r$,
\[\#\{(a,b):\varphi(ab)=d,\ a=r\}=\#\{c\in G:\varphi(c)=d\},\]
which does not depend on $r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:transport} to $f(a,c)=[\varphi(c)=d\wedge a=r]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Posterior indifference]
For every reading $d$ and classes $r,s$, the number of pairs with reading $d$ and target in $r$ equals the number with target in $s$. Conditioned on any reading, the posterior over the target is uniform.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
For $G=(\Z/3\Z)^\times=\{1,2\}$ and $\varphi(c)=c$: if $c=1$ the pairs are $(1,1),(2,2)$; if $c=2$ they are $(1,2),(2,1)$. The target is $1$ or $2$ with equal probability in each case, although $c$ determines whether $a=b$.
\end{example}

\section{The keep-rate law}

\begin{theorem}[Keep-rate law]\label{thm:keep}
For every filter $K$, $\hit(K)=\sum_{c\in G}|K(c)|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}, $\sum_{(a,b)}[a\in K(ab)]=\sum_c\sum_a[a\in K(c)]=\sum_c|K(c)|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Real filter equals sham]
If $|K_{\mathrm{real}}(c)|=|K_{\mathrm{sham}}(c)|$ for all $c$, then $\hit(K_{\mathrm{real}})=\hit(K_{\mathrm{sham}})$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Success rate equals keep rate]
If $|K(c)|=k$ for all $c$, then $\hit(K)/|G\times G|=k/n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[No-fallback failure rate]
If $|K(c)|+1=n$ for every $c$, then $\#\{(a,b):a\notin K(ab)\}=n$; the failure probability is exactly $1/n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$\hit(K)=n(n-1)$, and $n^2-n(n-1)=n$.
\end{proof}

\section{Ordering invariance}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rank}
For a bijection $e:G\to\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, $\#\{x:e(x)\le e(a)\}=e(a)+1$, and $2\sum_a e(a)=n(n-1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Ordering invariance]\label{thm:order}
For every ordering policy, $2\sum_{(a,b)} r_{ab}(a)=n\cdot n(n-1)$. The expected target rank is $(n-1)/2$ and the expected number of classes examined is $(n+1)/2$, for every policy.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:transport} the sum is $\sum_c\sum_a r_c(a)$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:rank}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Any two ordering policies have the same total search cost.
\end{corollary}

\section{Honest cost accounting}

Fix an ordering policy and write $U_c(a)=\{x:r_c(x)\le r_c(a)\}$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{baseline cost} is $\base(c,a)=|U_c(a)|$. The \emph{honest filter cost}, pricing each membership test at one division and each kept division at one more, is
\[\cost_K(c,a)=\sum_{x\in U_c(a)}\bigl(1+[x\in K(c)]\bigr)=\base(c,a)+|U_c(a)\cap K(c)|.\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The $1\times$ cap]
For every filter and ordering policy, $\sum_{(a,b)}\base(ab,a)\le\sum_{(a,b)}\cost_K(ab,a)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Baseline total]
For every ordering policy, $2\sum_{(a,b)}\base(ab,a)=n\cdot n(n+1)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\base=\text{rank}+1$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:rank}); apply Theorem~\ref{thm:order}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $0.5\times$ law]
For the full-keep filter $K(c)=G$, $\sum_{(a,b)}\cost_K(ab,a)=2\sum_{(a,b)}\base(ab,a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$U_c(a)\cap G=U_c(a)$, so $\cost=2\,\base$ pointwise.
\end{proof}

A filter whose fallback eventually divides every tested candidate behaves as the full-keep filter, landing at exactly $0.5\times$; this is the observed $\approx0.50\times$. A constant-size filter keeping a fraction $\rho$ in random arrangement costs about $(1+\rho)$ times the baseline.

\section{Robustness and boundary}

\subsection{Sham co-inflation}

\begin{lemma}[Rank-sum identity]\label{lem:ranksum}
For a bijection $e:G\to\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and $T\subseteq G$,
$2\sum_{a\in T}\#\{x\in T:e(x)\le e(a)\}=|T|(|T|+1)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The left side is $\sum_{a,x\in T}([e(x)\le e(a)]+[e(a)\le e(x)])$; for $x\ne a$ exactly one indicator is $1$, for $x=a$ both are.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sham co-inflation]
If the membership test is left unpriced, so a successful run costs $|U_{ab}(a)\cap K(ab)|$, then
\[2\sum_{(a,b):\,a\in K(ab)}|U_{ab}(a)\cap K(ab)|=\sum_{c\in G}|K(c)|\bigl(|K(c)|+1\bigr).\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}, then Lemma~\ref{lem:ranksum} with $T=K(c)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Under this bug, the real filter and any same-size sham --- even with different ordering policies --- report identical total cost on successful runs.
\end{corollary}

With constant keep size $k$ the mean buggy cost per success is $(k+1)/2$, against a mean plain-scan cost of $(n+1)/2$, so the bug reports a speedup of exactly $(n+1)/(k+1)$ for every filter of that size. A shared ``speedup'' is therefore an accounting artefact. With $n=24$ and $k=8$, the bug reports $25/9\approx2.78\times$ for both filters.

\subsection{Arbitrary priors: $N$-blind optimality}

Let $\mu:G\to\R$ be any weight on target classes; only the cofactor remains uniform. Define $W_\mu(K)=\sum_{(a,b):\,a\in K(ab)}\mu(a)$.

\begin{theorem}[Weighted keep law]\label{thm:weighted}
$W_\mu(K)=\sum_{c\in G}\sum_{a\in K(c)}\mu(a)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[$N$-blind optimality]
For every filter $K$ there is $c_0\in G$ such that the $N$-blind filter $K'(c)\equiv K(c_0)$ satisfies $W_\mu(K)\le W_\mu(K')$. If $K$ has constant keep size $k$, so does $K'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $c_0$ maximizing $\sum_{a\in K(c)}\mu(a)$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:weighted} termwise.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The boundary: the unordered event}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{mirror} of $T$ at $c$ is $cT^{-1}=\{cx^{-1}:x\in T\}$. The \emph{unordered success count} is $\hit^{\mathrm{sym}}(K)=\#\{(a,b):a\in K(ab)\text{ or }b\in K(ab)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Unordered score]
$\hit^{\mathrm{sym}}(K)=\sum_{c\in G}|K(c)\cup cK(c)^{-1}|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$b=a^{-1}(ab)\in K(c)$ iff $a\in cK(c)^{-1}$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
(a) $\hit(K)\le\hit^{\mathrm{sym}}(K)$. (b) If each $K(c)$ is mirror-closed the scores agree. (c) If $K(c)\cup cK(c)^{-1}=G$ for all $c$, then $\hit^{\mathrm{sym}}(K)=n^2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Boundary of the sham law]
In the cyclic group $C_3$, $K_1(c)=\{1\}$ and $K_2(c)=\{c^2\}$ have equal ordered scores ($3$) but $\hit^{\mathrm{sym}}(K_1)=5\ne3=\hit^{\mathrm{sym}}(K_2)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\{c^2\}=\{c^{-1}\}$ is its own mirror; the mirror of $\{1\}$ at $c$ is $\{c\}$, giving $1+2+2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Information: capacity orthogonal to the target}

All distributions are uniform on a finite sample space; $H$ is entropy in bits and $I$ mutual information.

\begin{lemma}[Flat readouts]
If $X$ takes values in $A$, each on exactly a $1/|A|$ share, then $H(X)=\log_2|A|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Flat posterior implies zero channel]
If on every fibre of $T$ each value of $A$ is taken on exactly a $1/|A|$ share of the fibre, then $I(X;T)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$H(X)=\log_2|A|=H(X\mid T=t)$ for every $t$; apply the chain rule.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero conversion]\label{thm:zero}
On $G\times G$, for every dial $\varphi$, $I(a;\varphi(ab))=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fibre at $d$ has $n\cdot\#\varphi^{-1}(d)$ elements, and each target class occupies $\#\varphi^{-1}(d)$ of them (Theorem~\ref{thm:flat}).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Batteries add nothing]
$I(a;\varphi_2(ab)\mid\varphi_1(ab))=0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Capacity is real but orthogonal]
$I((a,b);\varphi(ab))=H(\varphi(ab))$ and $I(a;\varphi(ab))=0$. In particular $I((a,b);ab)=\log_2 n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Public-ness audit]
(a) A dial $D(a,b)=\psi(a)$ read through the target satisfies $I(a;D)=H(D)$. (b) Any readout with $I(a;D)\ne0$ is not of the form $\varphi(ab)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
For $G=(\Z/3\Z)^\times$: $I((a,b);ab)=1$ bit, $I(a;ab)=0$, and the identity table read through the target leaks $I(a;a)=1$ bit --- the ``dummy dial v1'' failure.
\end{example}

\section{Methodology ledger}
Nine catches were logged. Two cost-accounting bugs produced spurious speedups above $1.5\times$; the sham displayed the same inflation, which by sham co-inflation identifies a keep-size-only artefact, and a derivation confirmed the error. A dummy dial leaked $1$ bit about the target; by the public-ness audit it could not be computable from $N$, and indeed it read its table through the factors. It was rebuilt as a public dial.

\section{Algorithms}
\textbf{Posterior-ranked filter.} Tabulate training counts $w_d(r)$ of targets in class $r$ with reading $d$; let $K(c)$ be the $k$ classes of largest $w_{\varphi(c)}$; trial-divide candidates $x\le\sqrt N$ with $x\bmod m\in K(N\bmod m)$, optionally followed by fallback.
\textbf{Same-size sham.} As above with $K(c)$ a uniform random $k$-subset.
\textbf{Exact keep-rate audit.} Enumerate the $n^2$ pairs, count $a\in K(ab)$, compare with $\sum_c|K(c)|$, and tally honest costs; $O(n^2)$ per policy.

\section{Discussion}
The $4/3\times$ prediction implicitly assumed that bits about the factor \emph{pair} could be spent on the \emph{target}. They are orthogonal. The theorems are exact under equidistribution; real semiprimes deviate by about $0.009$, consistent with the noise-floor discrepancy. The $N$-blind theorem already removes the assumption on the target. The results concern residue-driven static filters and orderings within trial division; they do not address methods that use $N$ differently, such as sieves.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \textbf{Approximate flatness:} if the cofactor law is $\varepsilon$-close to uniform in total variation, public-dial filters should beat the best $N$-blind filter of equal size by at most $2\varepsilon$ times the keep size.
\item \textbf{Non-abelian Frobenius filters:} with Haar-distributed cofactor Frobenius, success should equal keep size for class-function filters.
\item \textbf{Mirror-pair covering number:} the minimal full unordered cover should have size $\lceil(n+s(c))/2\rceil$, $s(c)$ the number of square roots of $c$, with ordered success still the keep rate.
\item \textbf{Adaptive filters:} under a flat posterior the expected cost should be at least $(n+1)/2$ even for keep-sets adapting to failed divisions.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
The map $(a,b)\mapsto(a,ab)$ is a bijection, so the public residue is independent of the target, the posterior is flat, success equals keep size, every ordering has the same expected cost, and under honest pricing no filter beats plain trial division while full-keep filters run at exactly half speed. Public dials carry their full entropy about the factor pair and zero bits about the factor trial division must find.

\end{document}

